1
00:00:25,826 --> 00:00:28,125
♪♪

2
00:00:36,403 --> 00:00:38,304
♪♪

3
00:00:49,449 --> 00:00:51,509
♪♪

4
00:01:18,812 --> 00:01:20,123
You're doing very well.

5
00:01:20,147 --> 00:01:22,225
A little bruising and
swelling are perfectly normal.

6
00:01:22,249 --> 00:01:23,626
Just continue taking it easy,

7
00:01:23,650 --> 00:01:25,695
and I'll see you in
about two weeks, okay?

8
00:01:25,719 --> 00:01:26,719
Thank you.

9
00:01:27,287 --> 00:01:30,533
I just want to die every
time I look in the mirror!

10
00:01:30,557 --> 00:01:32,769
How could he have
done this to me?!

11
00:01:32,793 --> 00:01:37,026
You'll feel better after
you talk to Dr. Weston.

12
00:01:38,398 --> 00:01:39,375
Please sit down.

13
00:01:39,399 --> 00:01:42,335
The doctor will be right in.

14
00:01:51,244 --> 00:01:53,304
Hello, Dr. Carr.

15
00:01:55,248 --> 00:01:56,248
All set?

16
00:01:56,683 --> 00:01:58,494
I'm ready if you are.

17
00:01:58,518 --> 00:01:59,747
Take a seat.

18
00:02:03,624 --> 00:02:06,594
Okay, I want you
to keep perfectly still.

19
00:02:49,369 --> 00:02:51,895
I think you're gonna
be very pleased.

20
00:03:00,447 --> 00:03:02,625
I want to see if I can
make the swelling

21
00:03:02,649 --> 00:03:05,194
under your chin
go down a bit faster.

22
00:03:05,218 --> 00:03:06,529
A couple of things I need.

23
00:03:06,553 --> 00:03:07,864
I'll be right back.

24
00:03:07,888 --> 00:03:08,888
Hey, Doc.

25
00:03:09,856 --> 00:03:11,154
You're a genius.

26
00:03:13,360 --> 00:03:14,726
Thank you.

27
00:03:19,533 --> 00:03:20,643
Just look at me!

28
00:03:20,667 --> 00:03:21,644
Look at my face!

29
00:03:21,668 --> 00:03:22,979
Miss Ferguson, what you see

30
00:03:23,003 --> 00:03:25,648
now is not what you're
going to see in six weeks,

31
00:03:25,672 --> 00:03:27,617
- I assure you.
- I wanted a nose

32
00:03:27,641 --> 00:03:29,542
like Elizabeth
Taylor's, now look.

33
00:03:30,410 --> 00:03:32,722
I am gonna hit you
with a malpractice suit,

34
00:03:32,746 --> 00:03:35,224
the likes of which
you have never seen!

35
00:03:35,248 --> 00:03:37,149
Miss Ferguson, please,
you're overreacting!

36
00:03:38,251 --> 00:03:39,844
Oh, what's the use?

37
00:03:43,924 --> 00:03:45,268
She's an emotional wreck.

38
00:03:45,292 --> 00:03:47,503
You should never
have operated on her.

39
00:03:47,527 --> 00:03:50,520
Maybe the way I screen
my patients leaves something

40
00:03:51,198 --> 00:03:52,496
to be desired...

41
00:03:53,734 --> 00:03:57,967
but it seems to me you
have the same problem.

42
00:03:59,072 --> 00:04:00,072
I do?

43
00:04:00,340 --> 00:04:03,868
We need to talk,
Lowell... tonight.

44
00:04:14,721 --> 00:04:16,899
That's all there is to it!

45
00:04:16,923 --> 00:04:19,736
I don't care what kind of
position this puts you in!

46
00:04:19,760 --> 00:04:21,804
You can't do this to me, Scott!

47
00:04:21,828 --> 00:04:23,206
Yes, I can. I'll be damned

48
00:04:23,230 --> 00:04:25,508
if I'm gonna let your
practice ruin mine!

49
00:04:25,532 --> 00:04:27,076
It's not gonna happen!

50
00:04:27,100 --> 00:04:29,879
Just as soon as I
talk to my attorney,

51
00:04:29,903 --> 00:04:32,600
this partnership is
going to be terminated!

52
00:05:17,450 --> 00:05:19,043
Good morning, Miss Ferguson.

53
00:05:19,486 --> 00:05:21,464
My name is Dr. Lowell Carr.

54
00:05:21,488 --> 00:05:23,389
I'm, uh, Dr. Weston's partner

55
00:05:23,490 --> 00:05:25,468
- at the plastic surgery clinic.
- Yes?

56
00:05:25,492 --> 00:05:26,803
I understand you're unhappy

57
00:05:26,827 --> 00:05:28,337
with the rhinoplasty
that he performed.

58
00:05:28,361 --> 00:05:30,106
I wondered if I might
have the opportunity

59
00:05:30,130 --> 00:05:31,574
to talk to you about it.

60
00:05:31,598 --> 00:05:33,042
You're not going
to change my mind.

61
00:05:33,066 --> 00:05:34,364
I assure you.

62
00:05:35,101 --> 00:05:37,002
This'll only take a moment.

63
00:05:38,104 --> 00:05:39,868
Your paper.

64
00:05:45,078 --> 00:05:46,979
Ooh, that coffee
smells wonderful.

65
00:05:48,415 --> 00:05:51,394
Would-would there be another
cup where that came from?

66
00:05:51,418 --> 00:05:52,418
Of course.

67
00:05:53,019 --> 00:05:55,011
Have a seat.

68
00:06:03,964 --> 00:06:05,262
Cream or sugar?

69
00:06:05,632 --> 00:06:07,624
Black's fine. Thanks.

70
00:06:16,076 --> 00:06:17,076
Thank you.

71
00:06:18,078 --> 00:06:21,446
All right.

72
00:06:23,483 --> 00:06:26,295
I'd like to propose that before
you take any legal action,

73
00:06:26,319 --> 00:06:27,530
you and I get together

74
00:06:27,554 --> 00:06:29,699
and see if we can find
a more constructive way

75
00:06:29,723 --> 00:06:31,316
to rectify the situation.

76
00:06:31,992 --> 00:06:33,324
What do you mean?

77
00:06:35,362 --> 00:06:38,025
Well, for instance, I
could reconstruct the nose.

78
00:06:38,365 --> 00:06:40,266
And if there's a problem

79
00:06:40,367 --> 00:06:42,268
with some other
anatomical feature,

80
00:06:42,669 --> 00:06:45,002
I could... I could
fix that, too.

81
00:06:45,205 --> 00:06:46,503
Are you saying

82
00:06:47,207 --> 00:06:49,518
that if I dropped
the malpractice suit,

83
00:06:49,542 --> 00:06:52,388
you'll give me a free
tummy tuck or something?

84
00:06:52,412 --> 00:06:54,724
My goal is to see you satisfied.

85
00:06:56,416 --> 00:06:57,960
I don't want to keep you,

86
00:06:57,984 --> 00:07:00,317
but why don't
you think about it,

87
00:07:00,553 --> 00:07:02,565
and I'll... I'll give you
a call this evening.

88
00:07:02,589 --> 00:07:04,717
I should be home around 7:30.

89
00:07:04,958 --> 00:07:07,086
No, I-I have a dinner tonight.

90
00:07:08,795 --> 00:07:10,773
Why don't you call
the office tomorrow,

91
00:07:10,797 --> 00:07:12,875
and I'll tell Lori to
squeeze you in this week.

92
00:07:12,899 --> 00:07:13,899
Okay.

93
00:07:15,902 --> 00:07:17,495
Thanks for the coffee.

94
00:07:17,904 --> 00:07:19,896
And thanks for coming by.

95
00:07:49,970 --> 00:07:51,962
♪♪

96
00:08:01,014 --> 00:08:02,312
Dr. Lowell Carr.

97
00:08:04,017 --> 00:08:05,017
Okay.

98
00:08:05,418 --> 00:08:07,410
Oh, thank you.

99
00:08:09,589 --> 00:08:10,589
Lowell.

100
00:08:10,890 --> 00:08:12,234
I just saw your piece

101
00:08:12,258 --> 00:08:14,403
on your new skin grafting
procedure in the <i>Journal.</i>

102
00:08:14,427 --> 00:08:15,725
It was terrific.

103
00:08:16,262 --> 00:08:17,506
Well, thank you.

104
00:08:17,530 --> 00:08:19,508
I'd like to see your
notes on the procedure.

105
00:08:19,532 --> 00:08:22,195
Well, I'll send them
over first thing tomorrow...

106
00:08:22,369 --> 00:08:24,847
If you agree that
we stop talking shop,

107
00:08:24,871 --> 00:08:26,415
and you buy me a drink.

108
00:08:26,439 --> 00:08:28,101
Deal.

109
00:08:58,905 --> 00:08:59,905
♪♪

110
00:09:04,911 --> 00:09:06,379
Miss Ferguson?

111
00:09:15,522 --> 00:09:17,514
Miss Ferguson?

112
00:09:19,292 --> 00:09:20,885
It's me, Dr. Weston.

113
00:09:54,160 --> 00:09:56,152
♪♪

114
00:10:19,385 --> 00:10:20,978
Yes, that's my knife,

115
00:10:21,054 --> 00:10:23,632
but I don't know how it
ended up in Dr. Weston's back,

116
00:10:23,656 --> 00:10:26,168
or how he ended up in
my house, for that matter.

117
00:10:26,192 --> 00:10:28,204
We found this in his pocket.

118
00:10:28,228 --> 00:10:30,891
It seems someone named
Doreen Ferguson called him

119
00:10:31,397 --> 00:10:32,942
at 4:30 this afternoon,

120
00:10:32,966 --> 00:10:35,211
told him to come to this
address at 7:15 tonight.

121
00:10:35,235 --> 00:10:37,379
I didn't leave a
message like that.

122
00:10:37,403 --> 00:10:38,647
But you are one of his patients.

123
00:10:38,671 --> 00:10:39,671
Was.

124
00:10:39,973 --> 00:10:41,271
I was, yes.

125
00:10:41,374 --> 00:10:43,285
I went to him for a nose job.

126
00:10:43,309 --> 00:10:45,121
I wanted him to
make my nose smaller

127
00:10:45,145 --> 00:10:47,356
and make it go
up instead of down,

128
00:10:47,380 --> 00:10:50,526
but thanks to that charlatan,
I went from having a beak

129
00:10:50,550 --> 00:10:52,143
to having a snout!

130
00:10:52,552 --> 00:10:53,850
Oh, my God.

131
00:10:54,888 --> 00:10:56,789
You think I killed him.

132
00:10:56,990 --> 00:10:59,118
Well, for the sake of argument,

133
00:10:59,492 --> 00:11:01,688
where were you
around 7:15 tonight?

134
00:11:02,729 --> 00:11:03,729
I was...

135
00:11:04,898 --> 00:11:07,877
closing up the antique store I
work at down on Ballard Street.

136
00:11:07,901 --> 00:11:10,564
- Anybody see you?
- You bet someone saw me.

137
00:11:10,737 --> 00:11:12,281
The cretin who rear-ended me

138
00:11:12,305 --> 00:11:13,916
when I pulled out
of the parking lot.

139
00:11:13,940 --> 00:11:14,940
In fact...

140
00:11:16,476 --> 00:11:18,377
here... here is his name.

141
00:11:19,646 --> 00:11:21,190
Here is his phone number.

142
00:11:21,214 --> 00:11:22,758
Here is his insurance company.

143
00:11:22,782 --> 00:11:24,080
Oh, oh, and...

144
00:11:24,817 --> 00:11:26,718
here is the badge number

145
00:11:27,820 --> 00:11:29,413
of the police officer

146
00:11:29,489 --> 00:11:31,567
who stopped to make
sure everything was okay.

147
00:11:31,591 --> 00:11:32,889
Any other questions?

148
00:11:33,626 --> 00:11:34,626
Nope.

149
00:11:35,562 --> 00:11:36,562
- Doreen?
- Ellie.

150
00:11:37,564 --> 00:11:38,941
That's my next-door neighbor.

151
00:11:38,965 --> 00:11:39,965
Jim.

152
00:11:39,999 --> 00:11:40,999
Okay.

153
00:11:42,001 --> 00:11:43,594
Are you all right?

154
00:11:44,137 --> 00:11:45,137
What happened?

155
00:11:46,139 --> 00:11:47,437
That's Dr. Weston.

156
00:11:47,874 --> 00:11:50,853
Someone murdered him in my
home to make it look like I did it.

157
00:11:50,877 --> 00:11:52,470
Oh, my God, Doreen!

158
00:11:53,546 --> 00:11:54,546
Miss?

159
00:11:55,048 --> 00:11:57,526
Do you remember seeing
or hearing anything unusual

160
00:11:57,550 --> 00:11:58,848
around 7:00, 7:30?

161
00:11:59,118 --> 00:12:01,019
Um, uh, oh, yes, actually.

162
00:12:02,622 --> 00:12:05,067
I-I saw a man go through
the door right about that time.

163
00:12:05,091 --> 00:12:06,202
Can you describe him?

164
00:12:06,226 --> 00:12:08,354
Um... he was the same man

165
00:12:09,095 --> 00:12:10,239
that was here this morning,

166
00:12:10,263 --> 00:12:11,874
you know, right before
you went to work.

167
00:12:11,898 --> 00:12:13,209
We keep an eye on each other.

168
00:12:13,233 --> 00:12:15,511
You know, attractive
single women living alone

169
00:12:15,535 --> 00:12:16,879
cannot be too careful.

170
00:12:16,903 --> 00:12:19,236
Who would that have
been, Miss Ferguson?

171
00:12:19,672 --> 00:12:20,672
Dr. Carr.

172
00:12:22,275 --> 00:12:24,938
I was at her house
yesterday morning, yes,

173
00:12:25,111 --> 00:12:27,012
but not last night, no.

174
00:12:28,414 --> 00:12:30,747
But were you at home last night?

175
00:12:30,950 --> 00:12:33,943
No, I-I was at the Southern
Association of Physicians'

176
00:12:34,320 --> 00:12:36,165
dinner meeting at
the Americana Hotel

177
00:12:36,189 --> 00:12:38,317
with a couple
hundred other doctors.

178
00:12:38,758 --> 00:12:40,603
In fact, I was holding
a seat for Scott;

179
00:12:40,627 --> 00:12:41,637
he said he'd be late.

180
00:12:41,661 --> 00:12:43,254
He... he never showed.

181
00:12:44,430 --> 00:12:45,975
Now I know why.

182
00:12:45,999 --> 00:12:47,243
You two get along?

183
00:12:47,267 --> 00:12:48,565
Well, we're friends.

184
00:12:49,202 --> 00:12:50,500
Uh, Bob,

185
00:12:51,204 --> 00:12:54,197
Lowell's worked quite a
few miracles in his career,

186
00:12:54,707 --> 00:12:57,267
but even he can't be
in two places at once.

187
00:12:58,711 --> 00:12:59,711
Yeah.

188
00:13:00,046 --> 00:13:01,323
Well, it was dark.

189
00:13:01,347 --> 00:13:03,626
The neighbor must have
mistaken him for somebody else.

190
00:13:03,650 --> 00:13:04,650
Yeah.

191
00:13:04,851 --> 00:13:06,495
I-I can go back to my office?

192
00:13:06,519 --> 00:13:07,596
Sure. I'll even drive you.

193
00:13:18,598 --> 00:13:19,896
You wear sunblock?

194
00:13:20,600 --> 00:13:22,778
Do I look like I'm
going to the beach?

195
00:13:22,802 --> 00:13:24,647
Dr. Carr took something

196
00:13:24,671 --> 00:13:26,749
off my cheek a
couple months back.

197
00:13:26,773 --> 00:13:28,617
It wasn't skin cancer,
but it could have been.

198
00:13:28,641 --> 00:13:30,234
Ever since then, sunblock.

199
00:13:30,310 --> 00:13:31,920
Eh, the ozone layer's
gonna make a comeback.

200
00:13:31,944 --> 00:13:34,379
You watch.

201
00:13:52,265 --> 00:13:54,166
That's Gary; he's my brother.

202
00:13:54,567 --> 00:13:55,830
We're twins.

203
00:14:08,181 --> 00:14:10,359
Lowell left a message
on my answering machine,

204
00:14:10,383 --> 00:14:12,961
saying the police were
taking him for questioning,

205
00:14:12,985 --> 00:14:16,319
so I got down here
as fast as I could.

206
00:14:16,989 --> 00:14:18,890
Are you a doctor also?

207
00:14:19,192 --> 00:14:22,794
Oh, no, ma'am, I... I
own a tune-up shop.

208
00:14:23,696 --> 00:14:24,994
We doctor cars.

209
00:14:25,998 --> 00:14:28,490
And did you know
the victim Dr. Weston?

210
00:14:29,535 --> 00:14:30,833
Yes, of course.

211
00:14:31,871 --> 00:14:35,205
Uh, did you mean to ask
Gary here that question?

212
00:14:36,075 --> 00:14:37,373
Oh, <i>you're</i> Gary?

213
00:14:37,477 --> 00:14:39,810
Very few people
can keep us straight.

214
00:14:40,279 --> 00:14:42,180
Yes, uh, let's see, um,

215
00:14:42,815 --> 00:14:44,943
when did you arrive at the...

216
00:14:45,818 --> 00:14:48,811
at the Southern Association of
Physicians' meeting last night?

217
00:14:50,223 --> 00:14:52,351
I wasn't at the
Southern Association

218
00:14:52,492 --> 00:14:53,790
of Physicians' meeting.

219
00:14:56,262 --> 00:14:59,824
Dr. Carr, would you
please stand up?

220
00:15:02,702 --> 00:15:03,702
Thank you.

221
00:15:04,937 --> 00:15:05,937
Now, Gary,

222
00:15:06,239 --> 00:15:08,572
did you know the
victim Dr. Weston?

223
00:15:08,808 --> 00:15:10,936
Well, no, no, not really, no.

224
00:15:12,078 --> 00:15:15,057
Where were you from
7:00 to 8:00 last night?

225
00:15:15,081 --> 00:15:17,259
I was in my office
at the tune-up shop

226
00:15:17,283 --> 00:15:19,184
with one of my suppliers.

227
00:15:19,519 --> 00:15:20,519
Thank you.

228
00:15:21,354 --> 00:15:23,550
Now, Lowell...

229
00:15:24,657 --> 00:15:27,650
where were you from
7:00 to 8:00 last night?

230
00:15:27,927 --> 00:15:31,473
I was having drinks in the
ballroom of the Americana Hotel.

231
00:15:31,497 --> 00:15:34,831
Your nurse told the police
that she overheard an argument

232
00:15:36,469 --> 00:15:39,548
between you and Dr. Weston
the night before the murder...

233
00:15:39,572 --> 00:15:44,169
Something concerning the
breakup of your partnership.

234
00:15:46,212 --> 00:15:48,545
It was a discussion,
not an argument.

235
00:15:49,148 --> 00:15:51,811
Well, it was a heated
discussion, wasn't it?

236
00:15:52,351 --> 00:15:53,944
A heated discussion, yes.

237
00:15:55,721 --> 00:15:57,849
Oh, you can sit down now.

238
00:15:59,158 --> 00:16:01,491
I don't have any more
questions, Lieutenant.

239
00:16:01,994 --> 00:16:03,839
However, I would like
to speak with Mr. Matlock

240
00:16:03,863 --> 00:16:06,924
outside, please.

241
00:16:09,936 --> 00:16:12,269
I have never seen
anything like this.

242
00:16:12,305 --> 00:16:13,282
Oh, I know.

243
00:16:13,306 --> 00:16:14,899
Lowell committed that murder.

244
00:16:15,274 --> 00:16:17,607
It's perfect; he's
got an identical twin.

245
00:16:18,978 --> 00:16:21,023
Nobody is gonna be
able to say for sure

246
00:16:21,047 --> 00:16:22,825
who came out of that house.

247
00:16:22,849 --> 00:16:23,478
You think so?

248
00:16:23,502 --> 00:16:25,461
And then he tries to
frame somebody to boot,

249
00:16:25,485 --> 00:16:28,197
just... oh, just making a
mockery out of the system.

250
00:16:28,221 --> 00:16:29,665
I really hate that.

251
00:16:29,689 --> 00:16:31,733
Julie, the man's
got an airtight alibi.

252
00:16:31,757 --> 00:16:33,035
Oh, come on, Ben.

253
00:16:33,059 --> 00:16:36,171
It's easy for somebody
to slip out of a party

254
00:16:36,195 --> 00:16:38,323
and then slip back in unnoticed.

255
00:16:38,364 --> 00:16:40,876
You're the one who's always
pointing that out in court.

256
00:16:40,900 --> 00:16:43,233
And even... even
if there were people

257
00:16:43,536 --> 00:16:45,180
with Lowell at that dinner,

258
00:16:45,204 --> 00:16:47,449
how do we know it was
Lowell and not his brother?

259
00:16:47,473 --> 00:16:48,584
W-Well, h-how do we know

260
00:16:48,608 --> 00:16:50,941
the person the neighbor
saw was Lowell?

261
00:16:51,277 --> 00:16:53,355
Using that logic, it
could have been Gary.

262
00:16:53,379 --> 00:16:56,042
No, no, Gary didn't
have motive; Lowell did.

263
00:16:57,283 --> 00:16:58,660
Julie, I just have a problem...

264
00:16:58,684 --> 00:17:02,348
Ben, Ben, you and I have
spent our entire working lives

265
00:17:03,489 --> 00:17:06,823
as officers of the court,
upholding the system of justice.

266
00:17:07,026 --> 00:17:08,927
I'm sorry, but that man

267
00:17:09,562 --> 00:17:14,296
is making fools out of us...
and I'm going to prove it.

268
00:17:29,148 --> 00:17:31,560
But did Gary ever
speak to Dr. Weston?

269
00:17:31,584 --> 00:17:33,362
Well, he may say hi to Scott

270
00:17:33,386 --> 00:17:35,631
when he'd come to pick
me up for lunch or something,

271
00:17:35,655 --> 00:17:36,865
but that was about it.

272
00:17:36,889 --> 00:17:38,482
Huh, they didn't socialize?

273
00:17:38,758 --> 00:17:39,758
No, never.

274
00:17:40,059 --> 00:17:42,404
Why are you asking me all
these questions about Gary?

275
00:17:42,428 --> 00:17:45,207
Well, if the neighbor
thought she saw you coming

276
00:17:45,231 --> 00:17:48,310
out of that house, it
could have been your twin.

277
00:17:48,334 --> 00:17:49,334
No, never.

278
00:17:49,602 --> 00:17:52,401
My brother couldn't
commit murder.

279
00:18:25,738 --> 00:18:27,866
Well, we're in the DA's hands.

280
00:18:28,240 --> 00:18:30,085
We'll have to see
what she's got.

281
00:18:30,109 --> 00:18:31,702
Boy, this is something.

282
00:18:32,511 --> 00:18:33,956
And you did this?

283
00:18:33,980 --> 00:18:35,357
Yes, I did.

284
00:18:35,381 --> 00:18:36,974
You're pretty good.

285
00:18:37,516 --> 00:18:39,417
Bet you're pretty
expensive, too.

286
00:18:39,986 --> 00:18:41,579
Hmm, so are you.

287
00:18:42,088 --> 00:18:43,088
Huh.

288
00:18:43,656 --> 00:18:45,215
Maybe we could work out a trade.

289
00:18:48,260 --> 00:18:50,593
You... you mean
your services for mine?

290
00:18:51,097 --> 00:18:53,225
Well, physical appearances
are not inconsequential

291
00:18:53,332 --> 00:18:54,810
in your line of work.

292
00:18:54,834 --> 00:18:57,827
Well, I've always been quite
satisfied with my appearance.

293
00:18:59,372 --> 00:19:00,965
Oh, don't take offense.

294
00:19:01,107 --> 00:19:02,317
As we say in my line of work,

295
00:19:02,341 --> 00:19:04,219
a little lift gives
you quite a lift.

296
00:19:04,243 --> 00:19:05,243
Hmm, hmm.

297
00:19:05,978 --> 00:19:07,422
Why don't you
take the book home,

298
00:19:07,446 --> 00:19:08,857
look at the before
and after pictures,

299
00:19:08,881 --> 00:19:11,874
and let's see if it
gives you some ideas?

300
00:19:12,251 --> 00:19:16,621
Well... okay.

301
00:19:34,206 --> 00:19:35,206
Hi, Benj.

302
00:19:35,541 --> 00:19:36,541
Oh, Les.

303
00:19:37,043 --> 00:19:38,636
- Coming or going?
- Going.

304
00:19:40,846 --> 00:19:41,846
What's this?

305
00:19:42,081 --> 00:19:44,826
Oh, it's a book this
plastic surgeon gave me.

306
00:19:44,850 --> 00:19:47,843
I'm representing him.

307
00:19:53,959 --> 00:19:55,257
Well, you know,

308
00:19:55,628 --> 00:19:59,156
it might not be a
bad idea at that.

309
00:20:00,199 --> 00:20:01,199
What?

310
00:20:02,068 --> 00:20:03,661
Well, look at yourself.

311
00:20:04,403 --> 00:20:06,304
Look at those crow's feet,

312
00:20:06,405 --> 00:20:08,183
the lines on your face.

313
00:20:08,207 --> 00:20:09,800
You're getting there, pal.

314
00:20:09,875 --> 00:20:11,173
Couple more years.

315
00:20:12,144 --> 00:20:13,388
You think so?

316
00:20:13,412 --> 00:20:14,412
Oh, yeah.

317
00:20:15,181 --> 00:20:18,845
Now, I, on the other hand,
will never need plastic surgery

318
00:20:19,018 --> 00:20:20,611
because I'll never wrinkle.

319
00:20:20,720 --> 00:20:21,720
Why?

320
00:20:22,421 --> 00:20:23,421
Skin pigmentation.

321
00:20:24,790 --> 00:20:27,783
See, I happen to have this
yellowish skin pigmentation

322
00:20:29,528 --> 00:20:31,656
that blocks out the sun's rays.

323
00:20:32,398 --> 00:20:34,299
So... I'll never wrinkle.

324
00:20:35,935 --> 00:20:36,935
Huh.

325
00:20:37,169 --> 00:20:38,680
You know, it's-it's true,

326
00:20:38,704 --> 00:20:41,037
there are people with
a-a yellowish pigmentation.

327
00:20:41,774 --> 00:20:42,774
It's natural,

328
00:20:43,075 --> 00:20:45,067
but Les...

329
00:20:46,112 --> 00:20:47,410
your yellowish pigmentation

330
00:20:49,181 --> 00:20:51,309
could be a sign of jaundice.

331
00:20:51,383 --> 00:20:53,284
What are you talking about?

332
00:20:54,086 --> 00:20:57,420
Jaundice, an-and the
yellow is not just in the skin.

333
00:20:57,723 --> 00:20:59,134
It's in the whites of the eyes.

334
00:20:59,158 --> 00:21:00,158
Let's see.

335
00:21:02,795 --> 00:21:03,795
Yep.

336
00:21:05,064 --> 00:21:07,056
Have you had your
liver checked lately?

337
00:21:08,200 --> 00:21:10,101
See, jaundice is a symptom.

338
00:21:10,636 --> 00:21:13,014
It could be a sign of
something else wrong with you...

339
00:21:13,038 --> 00:21:14,631
Anemia, poor circulation, or...

340
00:21:16,108 --> 00:21:17,108
pneumonia.

341
00:21:18,811 --> 00:21:21,144
It's probably nothing
to worry about, but...

342
00:21:21,480 --> 00:21:23,658
why don't we look
in the phone book,

343
00:21:23,682 --> 00:21:26,862
see if we can find a
specialist on liver diseases?

344
00:21:26,886 --> 00:21:29,014
You'll get checked
out and then...

345
00:21:29,522 --> 00:21:32,321
you will be...

346
00:21:38,130 --> 00:21:40,142
Yeah, I need a set of
brake lines over here.

347
00:21:42,935 --> 00:21:45,547
Yeah, this looks like a
bum alternator to me.

348
00:21:48,841 --> 00:21:50,952
We're going to have to go ahead

349
00:21:50,976 --> 00:21:53,455
and bleed that master
cylinder out on the clutch.

350
00:21:53,479 --> 00:21:54,479
All right.

351
00:21:55,714 --> 00:21:58,377
How long you been in
the tune-up business?

352
00:21:58,918 --> 00:22:00,529
Close to a year now.

353
00:22:00,553 --> 00:22:01,530
Doing all right?

354
00:22:01,554 --> 00:22:02,597
- Oh, yeah.
- Ah.

355
00:22:02,621 --> 00:22:04,032
Y-You want a soda or something?

356
00:22:04,056 --> 00:22:05,354
Uh... n-no, thanks.

357
00:22:06,492 --> 00:22:08,837
I understand before that, it
was the ice cream business.

358
00:22:08,861 --> 00:22:09,861
Yeah.

359
00:22:10,129 --> 00:22:12,641
And-and before that,
you owned a dry cleaners?

360
00:22:12,665 --> 00:22:13,809
Yes, sir, I did.

361
00:22:13,833 --> 00:22:16,826
And before that, it was
a Christmas tree farm.

362
00:22:17,570 --> 00:22:20,563
Yeah, I've been having a
little trouble finding myself.

363
00:22:20,973 --> 00:22:25,240
Yeah, unlike your brother,
who made it right away.

364
00:22:26,478 --> 00:22:27,478
You jealous?

365
00:22:27,546 --> 00:22:29,447
No, no, I-I'm-I'm not the...

366
00:22:31,116 --> 00:22:32,494
I'm not the jealous type, no.

367
00:22:32,518 --> 00:22:33,495
W-We get along well.

368
00:22:33,519 --> 00:22:34,519
Oh.

369
00:22:35,554 --> 00:22:36,852
I understand, uh,

370
00:22:37,857 --> 00:22:40,502
you tried to borrow
some money from him

371
00:22:40,526 --> 00:22:43,071
a few months back,
and he turned you down.

372
00:22:43,095 --> 00:22:44,095
Yeah.

373
00:22:44,597 --> 00:22:46,107
How did he turn you down?

374
00:22:46,131 --> 00:22:49,044
Well, he gave me that speech
on the value of the dollar

375
00:22:49,068 --> 00:22:50,969
and how adversity
builds character,

376
00:22:51,270 --> 00:22:52,480
and he was right, of course.

377
00:22:52,504 --> 00:22:53,504
Yes.

378
00:22:53,672 --> 00:22:56,005
So where'd you
finally get the money?

379
00:22:56,442 --> 00:22:57,637
Scott Weston?

380
00:22:58,677 --> 00:22:59,975
Well, no, I...

381
00:23:02,248 --> 00:23:04,581
I mean, I-I hardly
knew Dr. Weston.

382
00:23:06,852 --> 00:23:08,830
You, uh, s-see
that hat over there?

383
00:23:08,854 --> 00:23:11,666
It was left here by
a supplier of mine,

384
00:23:11,690 --> 00:23:13,591
a gentleman named Duke McKenna.

385
00:23:15,194 --> 00:23:18,707
We had a meeting the night
that Dr. Weston was killed.

386
00:23:18,731 --> 00:23:19,875
I-If you don't believe me,

387
00:23:19,899 --> 00:23:21,910
you can call him
up, you can ask him.

388
00:23:21,934 --> 00:23:22,934
Hmm.

389
00:23:25,204 --> 00:23:26,181
Well, maybe I will.

390
00:23:26,205 --> 00:23:27,205
Yeah.

391
00:23:27,539 --> 00:23:28,539
Yeah.

392
00:23:29,975 --> 00:23:33,605
So, you ever need
a tune-up... I'll call.

393
00:24:26,131 --> 00:24:29,124
♪♪

394
00:24:39,445 --> 00:24:40,956
Pretty good myself.

395
00:24:50,656 --> 00:24:52,648
How you doing, my friend?

396
00:24:54,693 --> 00:24:55,991
You look great.

397
00:24:56,962 --> 00:24:58,373
You came through for me, Gary.

398
00:24:58,397 --> 00:24:59,990
I'm grateful and impressed.

399
00:25:00,432 --> 00:25:01,432
Well, thanks.

400
00:25:03,369 --> 00:25:05,270
Does that mean we're square?

401
00:25:06,105 --> 00:25:08,074
You bet.

402
00:25:24,156 --> 00:25:25,749
Any time, my friend.

403
00:25:26,025 --> 00:25:27,960
Any time.

404
00:25:53,485 --> 00:25:55,613
Della, is my brother here yet?

405
00:25:56,055 --> 00:25:57,353
Here. I'm here.

406
00:25:57,389 --> 00:26:00,848
Come on, let's grab a sandwich.

407
00:26:07,299 --> 00:26:08,777
Is this your pen?

408
00:26:08,801 --> 00:26:09,801
No.

409
00:26:11,437 --> 00:26:14,100
I think you meant
to ask Dr. Carr.

410
00:26:14,873 --> 00:26:16,251
Is this your pen?

411
00:26:16,275 --> 00:26:17,819
Well, it... it could be.

412
00:26:17,843 --> 00:26:19,287
It's a company I
do business with.

413
00:26:19,311 --> 00:26:22,190
The sales rep often leaves
promotional items like this

414
00:26:22,214 --> 00:26:23,858
behind after he's made a call.

415
00:26:23,882 --> 00:26:26,628
Well, this pen was found on
the ground outside the window

416
00:26:26,652 --> 00:26:28,245
of Doreen Ferguson's home.

417
00:26:28,454 --> 00:26:31,099
The district attorney's
concluded that it fell out

418
00:26:31,123 --> 00:26:32,667
of the killer's pocket
when he entered the house

419
00:26:32,691 --> 00:26:33,835
through the window.

420
00:26:33,859 --> 00:26:35,760
I'm afraid you're under arrest.

421
00:26:35,961 --> 00:26:36,961
What?!

422
00:26:37,429 --> 00:26:40,108
You're gonna arrest him
because of some lousy little pen?

423
00:26:40,132 --> 00:26:42,033
- Gary, it's-it's okay.
- That's nuts!

424
00:26:42,534 --> 00:26:44,112
I mean, there must be
hundreds of pens like that.

425
00:26:44,136 --> 00:26:46,496
What, are you gonna go out
and arrest all those people, too?

426
00:26:53,812 --> 00:26:55,790
I'll take good care of him.

427
00:27:24,877 --> 00:27:26,470
Hi, Ben!
- Hi, Les.

428
00:27:26,845 --> 00:27:28,143
- Coffee?
- No, thanks.

429
00:27:31,383 --> 00:27:34,696
You son of a gun, you
really had me going yesterday

430
00:27:34,720 --> 00:27:36,018
with that jaundice.

431
00:27:38,991 --> 00:27:41,936
I went to my doctor, and
I got a clean bill of health.

432
00:27:41,960 --> 00:27:43,553
Oh, I'm glad, Les.

433
00:27:43,729 --> 00:27:45,857
Jaundice, you son of a gun.

434
00:27:47,799 --> 00:27:49,978
He did say I was a
little anemic, though.

435
00:27:50,002 --> 00:27:52,213
So he gave me
some iron pills to take.

436
00:27:52,237 --> 00:27:55,517
And I said, "I don't want
to take too much iron, Doc.

437
00:27:55,541 --> 00:27:58,204
I don't want to
clank when I walk!"

438
00:27:58,377 --> 00:27:59,675
He laughed.

439
00:28:00,746 --> 00:28:01,856
It's funny, yeah.

440
00:28:01,880 --> 00:28:04,008
Yeah, what's in the sack here?

441
00:28:05,083 --> 00:28:06,984
Oh, well, I-I don't understand

442
00:28:07,519 --> 00:28:10,465
why anyone would want to go
through the pain and expense

443
00:28:10,489 --> 00:28:13,152
of plastic surgery when
there's a simple solution.

444
00:28:13,559 --> 00:28:15,892
Look, guys wear
toupees, women wear wigs,

445
00:28:17,696 --> 00:28:19,289
false eyelashes and whatnot.

446
00:28:20,365 --> 00:28:22,410
Okay, you're going
out for the evening.

447
00:28:22,434 --> 00:28:23,978
What's wrong with this?

448
00:28:24,002 --> 00:28:25,300
- Turn around.
- Hmm?

449
00:28:26,371 --> 00:28:27,916
Well, go ahead,
turn around, will you?

450
00:28:27,940 --> 00:28:28,940
All right.

451
00:28:33,345 --> 00:28:35,177
Okay.

452
00:28:41,220 --> 00:28:43,121
Is this real or what?

453
00:28:43,455 --> 00:28:47,119
I mean, you can wear it all
evening. Who would know?

454
00:28:47,259 --> 00:28:48,259
Well...

455
00:28:50,229 --> 00:28:52,562
There's-there's one
problem I see right off.

456
00:28:53,732 --> 00:28:55,577
Your mouth doesn't
move when you talk.

457
00:28:55,601 --> 00:28:56,601
So what?

458
00:28:57,202 --> 00:28:59,314
Gregory Peck's mouth
doesn't move when he talks,

459
00:28:59,338 --> 00:29:01,204
and he gets away with it.

460
00:29:02,241 --> 00:29:03,241
Hi.

461
00:29:03,542 --> 00:29:05,443
- Well, hi.
- Conrad.

462
00:29:05,777 --> 00:29:07,322
Les, how are you doing?

463
00:29:07,346 --> 00:29:11,249
Oh, can't complain,
can't complain.

464
00:29:15,354 --> 00:29:16,947
Well, I'd better go.

465
00:29:17,489 --> 00:29:19,617
- Oh, don't rush off.
- Stick around.

466
00:29:19,858 --> 00:29:23,192
No, I got an appointment
to have my shoes half-soled.

467
00:29:23,562 --> 00:29:25,463
So, time is of the essence.

468
00:29:35,741 --> 00:29:38,074
- He was wearing a mask.
- I know.

469
00:29:38,543 --> 00:29:41,256
Have you ever thought
he was a little weird?

470
00:29:41,280 --> 00:29:42,578
Oh, yes, often.

471
00:29:43,348 --> 00:29:44,348
Yeah, yeah.

472
00:29:44,683 --> 00:29:47,016
- So, how'd it go?
- Oh, good, good.

473
00:29:47,986 --> 00:29:50,298
This morning I tailed
Gary from his tune-up shop

474
00:29:50,322 --> 00:29:53,401
to a bar on La Grande,
where he had a very brief

475
00:29:53,425 --> 00:29:55,103
but lively conversation
with this guy.

476
00:29:55,127 --> 00:29:56,425
Name's William Hodges.

477
00:29:57,129 --> 00:29:59,340
Been in town less than a month.

478
00:29:59,364 --> 00:30:00,957
Started managing the bar

479
00:30:00,999 --> 00:30:02,644
where he met with
Gary about a week ago.

480
00:30:02,668 --> 00:30:03,811
Know what they
were talking about?

481
00:30:03,835 --> 00:30:04,835
Couldn't hear.

482
00:30:05,103 --> 00:30:08,082
Is there some connection
between him and Dr. Weston?

483
00:30:08,106 --> 00:30:09,651
Not that I know of.

484
00:30:09,675 --> 00:30:10,885
I did find out
something interesting

485
00:30:10,909 --> 00:30:12,186
about the bar though.

486
00:30:12,210 --> 00:30:14,543
Used to be owned by Ken Groman.

487
00:30:15,347 --> 00:30:16,645
Oh, yeah, mobster.

488
00:30:18,116 --> 00:30:20,128
He disappeared about a year ago

489
00:30:20,152 --> 00:30:21,863
after being indicted
for racketeering.

490
00:30:21,887 --> 00:30:23,164
Who owns it now?

491
00:30:23,188 --> 00:30:25,089
Holding company, the
Guilfoyle Corporation.

492
00:30:25,924 --> 00:30:29,391
Now keep digging. Maybe... Yeah?

493
00:30:29,828 --> 00:30:32,821
Yeah, I was just on
my way down there.

494
00:30:34,199 --> 00:30:35,895
Run that by me again?

495
00:30:39,805 --> 00:30:43,799
Ben, I'm-I'm telling you, I was
given that pen after the murder.

496
00:30:44,543 --> 00:30:46,136
- Are you sure?
- Yes.

497
00:30:46,978 --> 00:30:50,972
Ask Lori, my nurse, or call
the sales rep and ask him.

498
00:30:51,650 --> 00:30:54,643
I'm absolutely positive
that he came to see me

499
00:30:55,187 --> 00:30:56,798
- the day after the murder.
- After?

500
00:30:56,822 --> 00:30:57,822
- Yeah.
- After.

501
00:31:00,459 --> 00:31:02,360
Someone must've stolen that pen

502
00:31:02,561 --> 00:31:05,895
and put it under that window
deliberately to frame you.

503
00:31:07,332 --> 00:31:11,360
- Really?
- Yeah.

504
00:31:28,954 --> 00:31:29,954
Hey!

505
00:31:30,589 --> 00:31:33,501
Oh, hey, I'm sor... sorry, I
got something in my contact.

506
00:31:33,525 --> 00:31:34,669
Let me buy you another.

507
00:31:34,693 --> 00:31:36,104
No, don't worry about it.

508
00:31:36,128 --> 00:31:38,106
Don't move! Please don't move.

509
00:31:38,130 --> 00:31:39,540
My contact fell out.

510
00:31:39,564 --> 00:31:42,110
You might step on it... here,
drinks all around, everybody.

511
00:31:42,134 --> 00:31:44,345
Just everybody, just
please stay in your seats.

512
00:31:44,369 --> 00:31:46,497
This should only take a minute.

513
00:32:14,132 --> 00:32:15,430
Hot dog. Yeah.

514
00:32:18,303 --> 00:32:20,204
"Seek and ye shall find."

515
00:32:23,909 --> 00:32:25,502
I'm sorry about that.

516
00:32:36,388 --> 00:32:39,381
He stopped by the day
after Dr. Weston died.

517
00:32:39,624 --> 00:32:42,617
I know because he
gave me a pen, too.

518
00:32:42,861 --> 00:32:45,006
I don't suppose either one
of you remember his name.

519
00:32:45,030 --> 00:32:46,623
Um, yes, Brian something.

520
00:32:47,399 --> 00:32:48,399
- Terwilliger.
- Yes.

521
00:32:49,468 --> 00:32:51,379
Hits on me every
time he comes in.

522
00:32:51,403 --> 00:32:52,403
- Oh.
- Well...

523
00:32:53,538 --> 00:32:55,131
the pen's not here.

524
00:32:55,340 --> 00:32:56,684
Thanks for your help, Lori.

525
00:32:56,708 --> 00:32:57,708
Yeah.

526
00:33:00,979 --> 00:33:02,572
So, now what happens?

527
00:33:04,015 --> 00:33:05,460
We go to trial.

528
00:33:05,484 --> 00:33:07,077
But we've just established

529
00:33:07,152 --> 00:33:08,629
that I couldn't have
dropped the pen

530
00:33:08,653 --> 00:33:10,731
on the night of the murder
because I didn't have it yet.

531
00:33:10,755 --> 00:33:13,401
Doesn't that blow a
hole in the DA's case?

532
00:33:13,425 --> 00:33:15,758
Lori heard you arguing
with the deceased

533
00:33:17,295 --> 00:33:18,888
shortly before the murder.

534
00:33:19,297 --> 00:33:21,976
And another witness said she
saw you coming out of the house.

535
00:33:22,000 --> 00:33:24,060
She's got a case.

536
00:33:26,404 --> 00:33:28,202
I see.

537
00:33:34,479 --> 00:33:36,072
Lowell, how you doing?

538
00:33:36,715 --> 00:33:38,013
Oh, hi. Fine.

539
00:33:39,084 --> 00:33:42,029
Um, Dr. Alvin Prescott, this
is my attorney, Ben Matlock.

540
00:33:42,053 --> 00:33:43,231
- Hello.
- I'm honored.

541
00:33:43,255 --> 00:33:45,066
Listen, I was just
in the neighborhood.

542
00:33:45,090 --> 00:33:47,568
I thought I'd stop by
and pick up the file.

543
00:33:47,592 --> 00:33:48,592
What file?

544
00:33:49,194 --> 00:33:52,373
The one that you promised
me at the Association dinner.

545
00:33:52,397 --> 00:33:53,808
Your notes on your new
skin grafting procedure.

546
00:33:53,832 --> 00:33:55,810
You said I could have them.

547
00:33:55,834 --> 00:33:56,834
Right. Right.

548
00:33:57,736 --> 00:33:59,580
Um, I'll run you off a copy.

549
00:33:59,604 --> 00:34:01,082
Why don't you stay right here?

550
00:34:01,106 --> 00:34:03,150
- Okay, I'll see you tomorrow.
- Yeah, right.

551
00:34:03,174 --> 00:34:04,174
Hmm.

552
00:34:04,910 --> 00:34:06,921
Truly, it has been an
honor meeting you.

553
00:34:06,945 --> 00:34:09,957
I'd like to think that
you are to criminal law,

554
00:34:09,981 --> 00:34:12,348
what I am to plastic surgery.

555
00:34:23,628 --> 00:34:26,962
At approximately 7:15, I saw
a man leave Doreen's house

556
00:34:28,099 --> 00:34:29,510
through the front door.

557
00:34:29,534 --> 00:34:31,746
Is the man you saw
in the courtroom today?

558
00:34:31,770 --> 00:34:33,898
Oh, yes, he's right over there.

559
00:34:34,406 --> 00:34:35,406
Thank you.

560
00:34:35,574 --> 00:34:36,940
Mr. Matlock.

561
00:34:38,977 --> 00:34:41,310
Um, Miss Stanford,
uh, are you sure

562
00:34:43,515 --> 00:34:46,027
this is the man
you saw that night?

563
00:34:46,051 --> 00:34:47,051
Positive.

564
00:34:47,319 --> 00:34:48,596
The porch light was on.

565
00:34:48,620 --> 00:34:49,897
I saw his face clearly.

566
00:34:49,921 --> 00:34:55,292
You're sure you saw this
man and not this man?

567
00:35:04,336 --> 00:35:05,929
Once again, Miss Stanford,

568
00:35:08,206 --> 00:35:10,641
whom did you see that night?

569
00:35:14,479 --> 00:35:15,777
I... don't know.

570
00:35:17,482 --> 00:35:18,814
Thank you.

571
00:35:31,663 --> 00:35:32,663
Redirect?

572
00:35:33,431 --> 00:35:34,729
Yes, Your Honor.

573
00:35:35,300 --> 00:35:37,963
Miss Stanford, what
did the man you saw

574
00:35:38,670 --> 00:35:40,748
do after he left your
neighbor's house?

575
00:35:40,772 --> 00:35:43,435
He got into a car
and drove away.

576
00:35:43,541 --> 00:35:45,286
And what did that car look like?

577
00:35:45,310 --> 00:35:49,304
Uh, it was maroon, one of those
new Japanese luxury sports cars.

578
00:35:50,015 --> 00:35:52,226
Is this the car he got into?

579
00:35:52,250 --> 00:35:53,843
Yes, that's the car.

580
00:35:53,885 --> 00:35:56,548
Let the record show the
witness positively identified

581
00:35:57,922 --> 00:35:59,300
a photo of the car

582
00:35:59,324 --> 00:36:01,657
owned by the defendant,
Dr. Lowell Carr.

583
00:36:02,327 --> 00:36:03,920
That's all, Your Honor.

584
00:36:04,062 --> 00:36:05,758
So noted.

585
00:36:30,822 --> 00:36:31,822
Ben?

586
00:36:32,123 --> 00:36:34,092
In here.

587
00:36:35,593 --> 00:36:37,186
- Hey.
- You got something?

588
00:36:37,429 --> 00:36:38,727
Do I ever.

589
00:36:39,397 --> 00:36:41,342
When I started checking
into the background

590
00:36:41,366 --> 00:36:44,545
of that guy that Gary Carr met
with, I came up with nothing.

591
00:36:44,569 --> 00:36:45,569
Zero.

592
00:36:46,204 --> 00:36:48,849
He's got no Social Security
card, no driver's license.

593
00:36:48,873 --> 00:36:51,001
- That's odd.
- That's what I thought.

594
00:36:51,376 --> 00:36:53,154
So, I stole a glass that
he'd been drinking out of,

595
00:36:53,178 --> 00:36:54,288
and I had them check the prints.

596
00:36:54,312 --> 00:36:55,556
What are you doing?

597
00:36:55,580 --> 00:36:56,580
Nothing.

598
00:36:57,649 --> 00:37:00,061
I-I just thought I saw
something on my face

599
00:37:00,085 --> 00:37:01,678
that wasn't there yesterday.

600
00:37:01,953 --> 00:37:03,698
It's probably just
a little something

601
00:37:03,722 --> 00:37:05,132
that comes with age, huh?

602
00:37:05,156 --> 00:37:06,156
Yeah.

603
00:37:07,292 --> 00:37:09,704
So, guess whose
prints were on the glass.

604
00:37:09,728 --> 00:37:11,026
- Whose?
- Ken Groman's.

605
00:37:12,931 --> 00:37:13,931
That's impossible.

606
00:37:14,799 --> 00:37:16,644
He disappeared a year ago.

607
00:37:16,668 --> 00:37:18,179
I know what Ken
Groman looks like.

608
00:37:18,203 --> 00:37:20,381
Everybody knows what
Ken Groman looks like.

609
00:37:20,405 --> 00:37:22,465
His picture was in
the paper for weeks.

610
00:37:26,811 --> 00:37:28,109
- Plastic surgery?
- Right.

611
00:37:29,414 --> 00:37:30,414
- Lowell.
- Yep.

612
00:37:31,216 --> 00:37:32,216
Oh, no.

613
00:37:32,383 --> 00:37:33,383
What?

614
00:37:33,685 --> 00:37:34,685
Dr. Prescott.

615
00:37:35,086 --> 00:37:36,086
Who's that?

616
00:37:36,454 --> 00:37:38,933
Someone who was
with Lowell at that dinner.

617
00:37:38,957 --> 00:37:41,602
He asked him to send him
something out of his files,

618
00:37:41,626 --> 00:37:43,037
only he never did.

619
00:37:43,061 --> 00:37:44,061
He didn't?

620
00:37:44,229 --> 00:37:45,229
No.

621
00:37:45,463 --> 00:37:47,591
'Cause Lowell
wasn't at that dinner.

622
00:37:47,799 --> 00:37:49,097
Julie was right.

623
00:37:50,201 --> 00:37:53,865
He was at Doreen
Ferguson's, killing his partner.

624
00:38:12,590 --> 00:38:13,888
Hey. Sleep much?

625
00:38:14,626 --> 00:38:17,004
No. I couldn't get
my mind off the...

626
00:38:17,028 --> 00:38:19,156
Dr. Weston must have found out

627
00:38:19,497 --> 00:38:21,625
that Lowell redid
Ken Groman's face,

628
00:38:22,367 --> 00:38:25,360
and that's why he wanted
to dissolve the partnership.

629
00:38:27,305 --> 00:38:29,283
So, what are you gonna do?

630
00:38:29,307 --> 00:38:30,851
Well, I was hired

631
00:38:30,875 --> 00:38:33,320
to give Lowell Carr the
best defense possible,

632
00:38:33,344 --> 00:38:37,839
so I guess I'll give
it a shot. Hmm.

633
00:38:41,719 --> 00:38:44,365
Mr. Matlock, you may
call your first witness.

634
00:38:44,389 --> 00:38:47,468
Your Honor, I call
Mr. Gary Carr to the stand.

635
00:38:50,762 --> 00:38:52,060
What's going on?

636
00:38:52,096 --> 00:38:54,088
You'll see. You'll see.

637
00:39:03,842 --> 00:39:05,435
Well, what's it like

638
00:39:07,045 --> 00:39:09,378
to be an identical
twin, Mr. Carr?

639
00:39:09,681 --> 00:39:13,675
Uh, well, I've just always
thought it was a fact of life.

640
00:39:13,785 --> 00:39:16,118
Yeah, you don't
mind being a twin?

641
00:39:16,421 --> 00:39:17,421
No.

642
00:39:17,655 --> 00:39:19,433
I should think
it'd be difficult.

643
00:39:19,457 --> 00:39:21,468
People always
trying to tell you apart.

644
00:39:21,492 --> 00:39:22,703
He's the smart one.

645
00:39:22,727 --> 00:39:24,038
He's the athletic one.

646
00:39:24,062 --> 00:39:25,306
He's the successful one.

647
00:39:25,330 --> 00:39:26,923
Doesn't it bother you?

648
00:39:26,998 --> 00:39:28,296
Well, sometimes, yeah.

649
00:39:28,499 --> 00:39:31,895
By the way, uh, which
one is the smarter?

650
00:39:32,136 --> 00:39:34,264
Well, I-I don't
know about smarter.

651
00:39:35,740 --> 00:39:38,403
Lowell usually got
better grades than I did.

652
00:39:38,877 --> 00:39:40,120
You the athletic one?

653
00:39:40,144 --> 00:39:42,272
Well, we were
both fairly athletic.

654
00:39:42,614 --> 00:39:44,742
He tended to play for string

655
00:39:44,983 --> 00:39:47,143
- more often than I did.
- You were the successful one?

656
00:39:48,820 --> 00:39:50,364
Well, no, not-not yet.

657
00:39:50,388 --> 00:39:52,516
I-I haven't really found
myself career-wise.

658
00:39:52,624 --> 00:39:54,752
Actually, you filed
for bankruptcy twice

659
00:39:55,493 --> 00:39:57,485
in the past seven
years, haven't you?

660
00:39:58,529 --> 00:39:59,529
Yeah.

661
00:39:59,731 --> 00:40:01,675
Has your brother ever
filed for bankruptcy?

662
00:40:01,699 --> 00:40:02,699
No, never.

663
00:40:03,434 --> 00:40:04,979
He's smarter than you,

664
00:40:05,003 --> 00:40:06,904
better at sports,
more successful.

665
00:40:07,005 --> 00:40:09,583
You resent the hell out
of your brother, don't you?

666
00:40:09,607 --> 00:40:10,851
Objection. Whatever

667
00:40:10,875 --> 00:40:12,019
ill feelings this witness may

668
00:40:12,043 --> 00:40:13,487
or may not have
toward his brother

669
00:40:13,511 --> 00:40:15,456
are not germane to this case.

670
00:40:15,480 --> 00:40:18,292
Uh, Your Honor, this
case rests on the fact

671
00:40:18,316 --> 00:40:20,661
that someone who looked
like my client was seen

672
00:40:20,685 --> 00:40:22,596
leaving the scene of the crime.

673
00:40:22,620 --> 00:40:25,283
Now, this witness's emotional
state is inarguably germane.

674
00:40:26,858 --> 00:40:27,858
Objection overruled.

675
00:40:29,627 --> 00:40:31,596
Please answer the question.

676
00:40:33,264 --> 00:40:35,597
Well, I'd be lying if I... said

677
00:40:36,501 --> 00:40:38,379
I didn't sometimes
resent him a little.

678
00:40:38,403 --> 00:40:39,996
You-you asked your brother

679
00:40:40,405 --> 00:40:42,349
to loan you $20,000
a few months ago,

680
00:40:42,373 --> 00:40:43,617
and he turned you down.

681
00:40:43,641 --> 00:40:44,852
That make you mad?

682
00:40:44,876 --> 00:40:46,174
For a while...

683
00:40:46,577 --> 00:40:48,705
It made you furious, didn't it?

684
00:40:48,813 --> 00:40:50,524
He humiliated you one more time,

685
00:40:50,548 --> 00:40:52,927
but this time, you were
gonna make him pay

686
00:40:52,951 --> 00:40:56,479
by framing him for a murder
that you committed, right?

687
00:40:58,389 --> 00:40:59,867
I didn't kill anybody.

688
00:40:59,891 --> 00:41:01,792
Act-actually, you
concocted two frames.

689
00:41:02,327 --> 00:41:04,805
You lured Dr. Weston to
Miss Ferguson's house,

690
00:41:04,829 --> 00:41:06,774
having heard that she
was upset with him,

691
00:41:06,798 --> 00:41:08,742
and killed him with
one of her knives.

692
00:41:08,766 --> 00:41:11,211
And then instead of leaving
by a window or something,

693
00:41:11,235 --> 00:41:12,746
you went out the front door,

694
00:41:12,770 --> 00:41:15,015
knowing damn well
the porch light was on.

695
00:41:15,039 --> 00:41:16,583
You knew somebody would see you.

696
00:41:16,607 --> 00:41:17,985
You wanted to be seen.

697
00:41:18,009 --> 00:41:19,219
You wanted somebody

698
00:41:19,243 --> 00:41:21,371
to positively identify
your brother's car.

699
00:41:21,646 --> 00:41:23,257
You wanted that pen to be found.

700
00:41:23,281 --> 00:41:25,192
You wanted the
police to conclude that

701
00:41:25,216 --> 00:41:27,328
your brother had
killed his partner.

702
00:41:27,352 --> 00:41:28,650
That's not true.

703
00:41:28,686 --> 00:41:29,897
You had keys to his house

704
00:41:29,921 --> 00:41:31,065
and his car, didn't you?

705
00:41:31,089 --> 00:41:32,089
Well, yeah...

706
00:41:32,523 --> 00:41:35,186
Knowing that he was at
that-that Association dinner,

707
00:41:36,761 --> 00:41:38,605
you went to the hotel
where it was being held,

708
00:41:38,629 --> 00:41:40,441
drove his car
from the parking lot

709
00:41:40,465 --> 00:41:42,076
over to Miss Ferguson's house,

710
00:41:42,100 --> 00:41:44,478
committed the murder
and drove back, didn't you?

711
00:41:44,502 --> 00:41:45,646
No. I-I didn't.

712
00:41:45,670 --> 00:41:47,014
I-I was at my shop all night.

713
00:41:47,038 --> 00:41:48,182
No, you weren't.

714
00:41:48,206 --> 00:41:49,799
I called Mr. McKenna.

715
00:41:50,208 --> 00:41:51,801
He left at 7:00,

716
00:41:52,076 --> 00:41:53,687
but then after he
left, he realized

717
00:41:53,711 --> 00:41:55,289
he's left his hat
in your office,

718
00:41:55,313 --> 00:41:58,025
so he drove back, got
back at 7:30, but he couldn't

719
00:41:58,049 --> 00:42:00,109
get his hat because you
weren't there, were you?

720
00:42:01,519 --> 00:42:03,831
I just went across the
street to get a sandwich.

721
00:42:03,855 --> 00:42:05,866
Light was out, the
door was closed,

722
00:42:05,890 --> 00:42:07,301
your car was gone.

723
00:42:07,325 --> 00:42:08,623
You weren't there.

724
00:42:08,793 --> 00:42:10,170
You were at Miss Ferguson's,

725
00:42:10,194 --> 00:42:12,220
framing your
brother, weren't you?

726
00:42:13,264 --> 00:42:15,256
I wasn't there.

727
00:42:23,141 --> 00:42:24,985
I was at the Association dinner.

728
00:42:25,009 --> 00:42:26,910
Oh, come on. Come on!

729
00:42:27,979 --> 00:42:29,880
To get into that dinner,

730
00:42:30,415 --> 00:42:32,748
you'd have to
show a positive I.D.,

731
00:42:33,051 --> 00:42:35,162
wear a name tag,
go by security guards.

732
00:42:35,186 --> 00:42:37,087
You weren't there. Lowell was.

733
00:42:37,255 --> 00:42:38,799
That wasn't Lowell. It was me.

734
00:42:38,823 --> 00:42:40,934
You'd say anything to
save your skin, wouldn't you?

735
00:42:40,958 --> 00:42:42,102
I can prove it. I...

736
00:42:42,126 --> 00:42:44,304
I-I can tell you who I
talked to, what I said,

737
00:42:44,328 --> 00:42:45,873
wh-where I sat, what
was on the menu.

738
00:42:45,897 --> 00:42:47,041
I-I can tell you everything.

739
00:42:47,065 --> 00:42:49,193
Ar-Are you asking this court

740
00:42:49,467 --> 00:42:52,801
to believe that your brother
planted a piece of evidence

741
00:42:52,937 --> 00:42:56,203
at the scene of the crime
to incriminate himself?

742
00:42:57,675 --> 00:42:59,086
I put the pen there.

743
00:42:59,110 --> 00:43:00,387
Gary, shut up.

744
00:43:00,411 --> 00:43:02,004
I did it...

745
00:43:03,414 --> 00:43:05,993
I did it because
Lowell told me to.

746
00:43:06,017 --> 00:43:09,229
He-he knew he'd be able to
show that it had been planted

747
00:43:09,253 --> 00:43:11,598
so people would think
that he'd been framed.

748
00:43:11,622 --> 00:43:13,955
I... I can't believe
what I'm hearing.

749
00:43:14,725 --> 00:43:16,570
Murdering Scott
Weston was his idea.

750
00:43:16,594 --> 00:43:17,971
It wasn't mine. It was his.

751
00:43:17,995 --> 00:43:19,206
It was his!

752
00:43:19,230 --> 00:43:20,528
Gary, stop it!

753
00:43:23,468 --> 00:43:24,468
Sorry. I...

754
00:43:26,204 --> 00:43:28,182
I don't want to take
the rap for this alone.

755
00:43:28,206 --> 00:43:29,206
Uh... Uh...

756
00:43:31,576 --> 00:43:32,874
You Honor, uh...

757
00:43:33,711 --> 00:43:36,374
could I ask for
a... a brief recess

758
00:43:37,582 --> 00:43:40,461
so that I could confer
with my client in private?

759
00:43:40,485 --> 00:43:42,386
Yes.

760
00:43:45,123 --> 00:43:46,967
What the hell are
you doing, Ben?

761
00:43:46,991 --> 00:43:48,335
Trying to defend you.

762
00:43:48,359 --> 00:43:50,260
Defend me?! You're burying me!

763
00:43:50,528 --> 00:43:52,861
I was trying to show
reasonable doubt

764
00:43:53,030 --> 00:43:55,742
by making it look
like Gary had done it.

765
00:43:55,766 --> 00:43:59,669
How was I to know that
you were in on it together?

766
00:44:01,139 --> 00:44:03,131
You did it, didn't you?

767
00:44:12,150 --> 00:44:13,727
Your partner found out

768
00:44:13,751 --> 00:44:16,448
that you operated on Ken Groman.

769
00:44:18,723 --> 00:44:20,300
You changed his appearance

770
00:44:20,324 --> 00:44:22,555
to keep him from
going to prison.

771
00:44:24,629 --> 00:44:28,293
The $20,000 that Gary wanted
from me, he got from Groman.

772
00:44:28,599 --> 00:44:30,192
Couldn't pay it back?

773
00:44:30,701 --> 00:44:33,034
If I didn't operate,
he'd kill him.

774
00:44:33,104 --> 00:44:34,104
Mm.

775
00:44:35,573 --> 00:44:37,166
Did you make Gary

776
00:44:38,709 --> 00:44:40,610
help you murder your partner?

777
00:44:40,845 --> 00:44:41,845
No.

778
00:44:42,880 --> 00:44:44,872
He wanted to.

779
00:44:46,117 --> 00:44:48,780
He said he got
me into this mess,

780
00:44:49,153 --> 00:44:51,748
so he should help me out of it.

781
00:44:53,858 --> 00:44:55,156
So what happens?

782
00:44:55,293 --> 00:44:56,591
He helps me.

783
00:44:57,428 --> 00:44:59,659
And screws up again.

784
00:45:07,138 --> 00:45:10,131
Your Honor, my client
wishes to change his plea

785
00:45:10,241 --> 00:45:12,142
from not guilty to guilty.

786
00:45:17,014 --> 00:45:19,142
That seems entirely
appropriate, Mr. Matlock.

787
00:45:19,750 --> 00:45:21,048
I'm revoking bail.

788
00:45:21,752 --> 00:45:23,345
I'm instructing the bailiff

789
00:45:23,554 --> 00:45:25,299
to remand the defendant's
brother, Gary Carr,

790
00:45:25,323 --> 00:45:26,600
to the lockup,

791
00:45:26,624 --> 00:45:29,287
and urge the prosecutor
to file charges immediately.

792
00:45:30,261 --> 00:45:31,559
Court is adjourned.

793
00:45:54,585 --> 00:45:56,918
Well, you sure
called that one right.

794
00:45:57,255 --> 00:45:59,366
What's more important...
I won the case.

795
00:45:59,390 --> 00:46:00,801
- Yeah.
- More or less.

796
00:46:00,825 --> 00:46:04,159
Yeah, and I lost my
shot at a free facelift.

797
00:46:04,228 --> 00:46:06,173
Are you thinking
about having a facelift?

798
00:46:06,197 --> 00:46:07,841
Well, it's like
Lowell Carr said:

799
00:46:07,865 --> 00:46:09,977
"A little lift gives
you quite a lift."

800
00:46:10,001 --> 00:46:12,879
Ah. I kind of like you
just the way you are.

801
00:46:12,903 --> 00:46:15,115
Yeah, but you're saying
that 'cause you won.

